<reference_puzzle index="1" source="train" puzzle_id="e84fef15" lines="39-59">
<description>
Reconstruct the repeated tile from its separated copies and mark the missing pixels in the damaged copy blue.

Input structure:
- The input is a lattice of equal-size blocks separated by one-cell solid rows
  and columns of the same separator color.
- All blocks are copies of one motif except exactly one block, where some
  motif pixels have been overwritten by the motif background color.
- Blue is absent from the input and is reserved as the output marker color.

Rule:
- Recover the motif by taking the unique majority color at each local block
  coordinate across all block copies.
- Find the single block that differs from this consensus motif.
- Return the consensus motif, recoloring the differing local coordinates blue.

Key insight:
- The repeated copies provide redundancy: the damaged positions are exactly
  the coordinates where the lone nonconforming copy has background instead of
  the consensus motif color.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="a416fc5b" lines="95-106">
<description>
Add the two missing colored arms of a centered diamond-sprite motif, or clear a completed motif to a blank square.

Input structure
An 11x11 board has orange background and pink separator bars forming nine 3x3 cells. Occupied cells contain a four-pixel diamond sprite; the center cell and one neighboring cell are red, and a completed board may also contain one grey and one teal sprite.

Rule
With only the two red sprites present, preserve the board and add grey and teal diamond sprites 135 degrees clockwise and counterclockwise from the non-center red arm. If those expected grey and teal sprites are already present, output an all-background square whose side length is the total number of sprite pixels.

Key insight
The non-center red sprite is an eight-neighbor direction from the center; the other two colored arms are fixed rotations of that direction.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="eval" puzzle_id="88e364bc" lines="102-115">
<description>
Slide yellow pixels inside walled regions in the direction dictated by border-legend arrows.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color; yellow pixels sit inside enclosed regions bounded by colored walls.
- Border legends: small rectangles on the grid edge whose fill color C matches a region's border color. Each legend contains a center dot and an indicator dot encoding a direction.

Rule:
- For each enclosed region, identify its border color C and look up the legend with fill color C.
- The direction from the legend's center dot to its indicator dot gives the slide direction.
- All yellow pixels in that region slide in that direction, stacking against the far wall.

Key insight: Legend fill color links to region border color; the center→indicator offset encodes the movement vector.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="train" puzzle_id="1e5d6875" lines="26-45">
<description>
Paint a recolored diagonal copy from each L triomino source object.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- The only foreground objects are 4-connected red and grey L triominoes,
  each occupying three pixels of a 2x2 box.

Rule:
- Grey L objects paint a yellow copy translated one diagonal step toward
  their missing 2x2 corner.
- Red L objects paint a green copy translated one diagonal step away from
  their missing 2x2 corner.
- Copies are clipped by the grid boundary; red-derived green copies are
  painted after grey-derived yellow copies where the projections collide.

Key insight:
- The missing corner of each 2x2 L object identifies its diagonal axis, and
  the source color determines which side of that axis receives the copy.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="train" puzzle_id="5e6bbc0b" lines="42-56">
<description>
Compact the checkerboard's moving color toward the edge marked by the teal pixel, leaving a maroon swept trail on the marker line.

Input structure
- A blue/black checkerboard with exactly one teal marker replacing a blue pixel on a non-corner edge.
- The marker's checkerboard parity identifies the moving color (blue); the opposite parity is background (black).

Rule
- If the marker is on the left or right edge, compact moving-color pixels in every row toward that side.
- If the marker is on the top or bottom edge, compact moving-color pixels in every column toward that side.
- Preserve the teal marker at its edge cell.
- On the marker's row or column, color cells swept by shifted moving pixels maroon unless the final compacted line occupies them.

Key insight
The marker selects both the compression direction and the checkerboard color class that moves; maroon records the movement trail only along that marked line.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="eval" puzzle_id="b5ca7ac4" lines="7-21">
<description>
Move bordered 5×5 squares to their designated grid edges by border color.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Azure (8) and red (2) bordered 5×5 squares with colored interiors, scattered across the grid.

Rule:
- Azure squares → left edge (col 0); red squares → right edge (col W−5).
- Each square keeps its original row.
- Process azure left-to-right, red right-to-left (nearest-to-edge first).
- If a square's rows overlap an already-placed edge square, place it one square-width inward instead.

Key insight: Near-edge-first ordering plus row-overlap determines which square sits at the edge vs. the second column.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="train" puzzle_id="2f767503" lines="66-81">
<description>
Erase each target-color object crossed by the ray pointed by the bar-and-control marker.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- There is one three-pixel straight marker bar and one singleton control pixel adjacent to the bar center.
- The remaining non-background color forms multiple 4-connected target objects.

Rule:
- Cast a straight ray from the marker bar center in the direction away from the adjacent control pixel.
- Find every 4-connected target-color object that intersects that ray.
- Recolor all pixels of those hit target objects to the background; all other pixels stay unchanged.

Key insight:
- The whole object is erased when the center ray touches any of its pixels; the full marker bar width is not swept.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="train" puzzle_id="46c35fc7" lines="21-34">
<description>
Rotate the colors around every 3x3 foreground ring, with corners and side-centers cycling in opposite directions.

Input structure
- The grid has a dominant background color.
- Each 4-connected foreground object is a complete 3x3 ring: eight foreground pixels around a background center.

Rule
- For each ring, move corner colors one step around the four corners.
- Move side-center colors one step around the four side centers in the opposite direction.
- Leave the background center and all other background pixels unchanged.

Key insight
- Corners and side centers form two separate four-cycles on the same 3x3 ring.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="train" puzzle_id="e3fe1151" lines="36-55">
<description>
Fill the one blank in each corner region so all four regions have the same color multiset.

Input structure:
- The dominant background color forms one solid separator row and one solid
  separator column.
- The separator cross splits the grid into four equal rectangular corner regions.
- Each corner region contains exactly one background-colored blank pixel.

Rule:
- Count the non-background colors in each corner region.
- The completed region multiset is defined by the maximum observed count of
  each color across the four regions.
- Replace each region's blank pixel with the single color needed to match that
  completed multiset.

Key insight:
- The separator color inside a corner region is a missing value marker; comparing
  the four regions reveals the shared multiset without using numeric color order.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="train" puzzle_id="c4d1a9ae" lines="28-48">
<description>
Fill each vertical foreground band's background cells with the color of the next band to its right.

Input structure
- The dominant color is the background.
- Each other color occupies a separate vertical band defined by that color's
  minimum and maximum columns.
- Within a band, all cells are either that band's color or background.

Rule
- Order the bands from left to right.
- In each band, replace background pixels with the color of the next band in
  that order, wrapping from the rightmost band back to the leftmost.
- Preserve all existing foreground pixels and all background pixels outside
  the bands.

Key insight
- The column bands form a cyclic palette legend: a band's gaps are completed
  by borrowing the color from its right-hand neighbor, not by changing the
  band's existing pixels.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="train" puzzle_id="7e2bad24" lines="91-106">
<description>
Extend the diagonal blue ray through the grid, reflecting it off red line-segment mirrors.

Input structure
- Black-background grid with one diagonal 8-connected blue ray seed.
- Red foreground objects, when present, are straight horizontal or vertical mirror segments.
- Other foreground bars are transparent to the ray and may be overwritten where the ray crosses them.

Rule
- Start from the ray seed endpoint whose outward diagonal extension remains inside the grid.
- Paint the ray continuously in that diagonal direction until it leaves the grid.
- If the next cell is a red mirror, do not paint it; reflect by flipping the row direction on a horizontal mirror or the column direction on a vertical mirror.

Key insight
- The non-red bars are decoys: only red bars reflect the diagonal path, while all other crossed cells become blue.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

